## Developer's Diary

When I first saw the task, my immediate thought was that `Array.filter()`
combined with `startsWith()` would be more than enough to get the job done.
It seemed straightforward. But then I re-read the requirements and realized
binary search was strictly required, not optional.

The problem was — even though I studied algorithms back in university,
I couldn't really recall the details when I needed them. So I went back
to basics, started reading about binary search theory, watched some YouTube
tutorials, and slowly it started coming back to me. It took more time than
I expected just to get comfortable with the concept again.

Once I understood the theory, my next struggle was figuring out where to
even begin with the implementation. I knew what binary search does in general,
but applying it to a prefix-matching problem felt like a different challenge.
I wasn't sure how to find not just the first match, but also where the matches
end. I got some direction from Claude which helped me think through the structure.

The key insight that unlocked everything was the `nextPrefix` trick — if the
prefix is `"ja"`, I increment the last character by one ASCII value to get `"jb"`.
Since the array is sorted, every word starting with `"ja"` must sit alphabetically
between `"ja"` and `"jb"`. So a second binary search on that upper bound gives
me the right boundary cleanly, and I can just slice between the two boundaries.

Being relatively new to JavaScript also added another layer of difficulty.
Some syntax and built-in methods weren't second nature to me yet, so even
translating the logic I had in my head into actual working code took extra
effort and patience.

But once everything clicked and the tests passed, it was genuinely satisfying.
I feel like I now understand binary search on a much deeper level than I did
after university — because this time I actually had to use it to solve a real problem.